{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"To solve the problem, we need to find integers \\( x \\) between 1 and 100 that satisfy the following system of congruences:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4} \\\\\nx \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3} \\\\\nx \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5}\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nFirst, let's use the third congruence:\n\\[\nx \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\nThis means that \\( x \\) can be written in the form:\n\\[\nx = 5k + 1\n\\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\).\n\nNext, substitute \\( x = 5k + 1 \\) into the second congruence:\n\\[\n5k + 1 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3}\n\\]\nSubtract 1 from both sides:\n\\[\n5k \\equiv 0 \\pmod{3}\n\\]\nSince \\( 5 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{3} \\), we can rewrite the congruence as:\n\\[\n2k \\equiv 0 \\pmod{3}\n\\]\nThis implies that \\( k \\) must be a multiple of 3. Therefore, we can write:\n\\[\nk = 3m\n\\]\nfor some integer \\( m \\).\n\nSubstitute \\( k = 3m \\) back into the expression for \\( x \\):\n\\[\nx = 5(3m) + 1 = 15m + 1\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute \\( x = 15m + 1 \\) into the first congruence:\n\\[\n15m + 1 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}\n\\]\nSubtract 1 from both sides:\n\\[\n15m \\equiv 2 \\pmod{4}\n\\]\nSince \\( 15 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4} \\), we can rewrite the congruence as:\n\\[\n3m \\equiv 2 \\pmod{4}\n\\]\n\n","prefix_end":1079,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":false,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":0.7,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0},"ids":[1249,11625,279,3491,11,582,1184,311,1477,25780,17767,856,1124,8,1948,220,16,323,220,16,15,15,429,26553,279,2701,1849,315,30169,84,2380,510,59,9640,59,7265,90,23910,532,87,1124,57317,220,18,1124,79,2593,90,19,92,90155,87,1124,57317,220,16,1124,79,2593,90,18,92,90155,87,1124,57317,220,16,1124,79,2593,90,20,532,59,408,90,23910,532,59,2533,5338,11,1077,594,990,279,4843,30169,84,763,510,59,9640,87,1124,57317,220,16,1124,79,2593,90,20,532,59,921,1986,3363,429,17767,856,1124,8,646,387,5326,304,279,1352,510,59,9640,87,284,220,20,74,488,220,16,198,59,921,1958,1045,7546,17767,595,112